krdpserver paints the host's pointer into the video for a client that
cannot draw one, and on request for every client. krdp sends a client
the pointer's shape to draw at its own pointer, so a viewer that only
watches never sees where the host's mouse is. A client that advertises
no pointer cache in its capabilities (pointer cache size 0) is one that
cannot draw shapes. Upstream means to refuse it ("Client doesn't support
pointer caching, aborting"), but that check runs in the Capabilities
callback, which FreeRDP calls before the server's Demand Active on the
server's own settings, so it never sees the client's value, and the
first cursor update then evicts from an empty cache in Cursor::update
and crashes the server. This patch reads the value in Activate, where
the client's capabilities have been applied, notes it on the connection,
and the session for it captures with the compositor's cursor painted
into the frames (KWin's screencast in its Embedded cursor mode, 2 for
the portal), then, once the client is streaming, sends it the null
system pointer so it draws none of its own. EmbedCursor=true in
[General] of ~/.config/krdpserverrc (Bool, default false) does the same
for every client, for a server that serves only watchers. Off, and for a
client with a pointer cache, nothing changes: krdp captures with the
cursor as metadata and sends shapes as before. KWin attaches no cursor
metadata in Embedded mode, so the null pointer has to be sent
explicitly; Streaming is set on the connection's own thread and may have
passed before the wrapper exists, so the wrapper both connects to
stateChanged and checks the state at once. The off path is upstream's
exactly: a false argument, two if (false) tests, and the unchanged mode
values. KRDC asks for this with its "Remote pointer" host option (krdc-
embed-cursor/ in this repository). Made against krdp 6.8.2.
server/krdpserversettings.kcfg is a symlink to the kcm's.

